./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000089-20260519-054228.log:1004:- Worker Dokploy import jobs (BE-0502 pattern for `import_dokploy_resource`) are organization-scoped only and use a typed payload `(organization_id, yalla_organization_id, dokploy_organization_id[, assignment_dokploy_org_id])`. The worker reloads the Yalla organization from Postgres, rejects stale desired versions before scanning Dokploy, then delegates classification/apply to `migrateimport.Importer` through a store-backed repository adapter. Each imported project/environment/service row and its `dokploy_refs` mapping are inserted in the same transaction, so replay after partial success classifies existing refs as `skip_already_imported` and creates only missing descendants. Scanner/Dokploy failures stay retryable unless cancellation interrupted the context; malformed/cross-tenant/stale desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000089-20260519-054228.log:1064:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000089-20260519-054228.log:1066:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. L