./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1008:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1024:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1031:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1083:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1084:- Cryptographic-primitive rotation contract pin (BE-0351 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{key_rotation_static_test.go, key_rotation_test.go} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/secrets/AGENTS.md "Key rotation" section; structural template for any future security-verification story over a long-lived cryptographic primitive that ships a rotation surface — JWT signing keys, webhook HMAC secrets, backup-target keys, mTLS issuance keys). Three load-bearing rules: (1) The cryptographic surface is pinned by a static AST analyser, not just by behavioural tests: parse the implementation file, locate the surface methods by name (`NewAESGCM`, `ActiveKeyID`, `KeyIDs`, `Open` for AESGCM), and assert each one's structural shape — `NewAESGCM` must take `[][]byte` (not a single key), `Open` must contain a range loop over the receiver's `keys` slice (not a single-key decrypt), `Open` must return the typed `ErrUnknownKey` identifier (not a string error). Behavioural tests catch the runtime regression but a static guard catches the silent refactor before the runtime test has a chance to fail. The self-check pins both directions of every analyser with synthetic regression sources. (2) The runtime test mirrors the operator runbook line-for-line as a three-phase rotation lifecycle: pre-rotation provider seals under k_old; mid-rotation provider boots with `[k_new, k_old]` and proves (a) old ciphertext still Opens via the retired key id, (b) new Seals reference the new active key id, (c) a fresh Seal under k_new does NOT decrypt under k_old's id (returns ErrInvalidCiphertext); re-seal step (the rotation worker's loop body) Opens under retired key then Seals under active and proves round-trip plaintext intact; post-retirement provider boots with `[k_new]` only and proves a row missed by the re-seal worker surfaces ErrUnknownKey (NOT ErrInvalidCiphertext, NOT a silent plaintext recovery). The runtime test reads as the runbook does so a future operator can compare the runbook against the executable contract. (3) The redacted-projection invariant is captured at runtime with a real `slog.NewJSONHandler` writing into a `bytes.Buffer`: `logger.LogAttrs(..., slog.Any("provider", provider))`, `json.Unmarshal` the result, assert `provider.master_keys == output.Sentinel`. The leak check substring-searches the JSON record for the FULL hex of every probe (k_old, k_new, plaintext, pre/mid ciphertext) — per-byte substring checks false-positive against the SHA-256-prefix key id (the 16-char id will collide with random 2-hex pairs in any 32-byte key at ~p=1/256 per byte). The same probes are checked against `fmt.Sprintf("%s", provider)` to pin the fmt.Stringer channel (panic messages). Both channels MUST also contain at least one accepted key id — the operator's only confirmation that a rotation took effect. (4) The store-layer routing index that the rotation worker depends on is pinned by a SEPARATE sealing trap in the migration package (the `migrations/` directory contains only .sql files — no Go package — so the trap lives in `internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go` and reads each migration via `fs.ReadFile(embeddedMigrations, ...)`). The regex matcher tolerates harmless variations (`IF NOT EXISTS`, optional whitespace, multi-line) but pins the load-bearing parts: index name, table, column order `(secret_provider, secret_key_id)`, and `WHERE is_secret = true` partial-index predicate. A migration that drops the WHERE clause widens the index from "secret rows only" to "every row", which changes the index's purpose and breaks the worker's bounded lookup; the self-check exercises eight known-bad shapes to pin this. The `requiredVariableMigrations` slice is the canonical set of `*_variables` tables — adding a new variable table without adding it here silently reduces coverage.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000012-20260518-170826.log:1090:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfigu