./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000019-20260518-211930.log:4565:ralph/prd.json:9577:      "notes": "Tenant isolation for quota_reservations. Implemented internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go covering 7 cross-tenant invariants on the QuotaRepository reservation surface: (1) InsertReservation cross-tenant bystander byte-identity — orgA's InsertReservation for the SAME resource orgB already owns on (active and committed peers across services/projects/databases) leaves every observable column on every orgB row byte-identical (id, organization_id, resource, amount, status, job_id, expires_at, settled_at, created_at, updated_at — updated_at is the load-bearing trigger anchor via quota_reservations_set_updated_at BEFORE UPDATE); (2) cross-tenant row-count invariant — orgA's InsertReservation on a resource orgB has not seeded changes orgA's count by +1 and leaves orgB's count untouched; (3) same-resource-and-amount in two tenants both persist as the positive-direction pair (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, resource); only PRIMARY KEY (id) and UNIQUE (organization_id, id) composite-FK target) — without this pair a regression resolving a phantom conflict by (resource,) alone would prevent orgA's INSERT and the bystander assertions above would silently pass against unmutated rows; (4) global PRIMARY KEY collision across tenants surfaces as typed apierr.Conflict (yerr.CodeConflict) through mapWriteError AND leaves orgB's row byte-identical AND inserts no orgA row — a cross-tenant requester cannot UPDATE orgB's row through an explicit-id collision; (5) SumActiveReservations tenant-scoping — orgA's sum on services = orgA totals only, orgB's sum on services = orgB totals only, even when both tenants hold rows on the same resource (10 vs 300 chosen so a cross-tenant fold would surface as 310); the orthogonal projects peer also guards the resource predicate; (6) SumActiveReservations on empty tenant returns 0 even when other tenants hold many rows on the same resource — defensive proof the organization_id predicate keeps cross-tenant rows out of the SUM source set; (7) ON DELETE CASCADE tenant isolation — deleting orgA via raw SQL CASCADE-removes orgA's quota_reservations rows and leaves orgB's three peers (active services, active projects, committed databases) byte-identical to baseline, mirroring TestQuotaUsageOrganizationDeleteCascadeIsTenantScoped (BE-0454). Helpers introduced: assertQuotaReservationByteIdentical + per-file ptr helpers stringPtrEqualQR/timePtrEqualQR/fmtStringPtrQR/fmtTimePtrQR (per-file QR suffix is mandatory because the package-shared store_test space already exposes QP-suffixed variants from BE-0451). All other helpers reused: seedOrg (schema_test.go), insertReservationRow/insertSettledReservationRow (quota_repository_test.go), loadQuotaReservationRowByID/countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg/runInsertReservationOrFail (BE-0455 invariants). All 7 tests PASS against an isolated migrated Postgres (race-clean, 2.83s). gofmt/vet clean. Pre-existing TestEnvironmentService*, TestAPIKeyServiceCreate*, TestProjectVariableService*, TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch*, TestServiceServiceCreate* parallel-induced flakes reproduce at HEAD without this change (22 failures with the file stashed; same set surfaces with the file in place; BE-0455's commit notes already documented this)."
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000019-20260518-211930.log:4584:ralph/progress.txt:166:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
