./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000039-20260518-235141.log:693:- Worker Dokploy import jobs (BE-0502 pattern for `import_dokploy_resource`) are organization-scoped only and use a typed payload `(organization_id, yalla_organization_id, dokploy_organization_id[, assignment_dokploy_org_id])`. The worker reloads the Yalla organization from Postgres, rejects stale desired versions before scanning Dokploy, then delegates classification/apply to `migrateimport.Importer` through a store-backed repository adapter. Each imported project/environment/service row and its `dokploy_refs` mapping are inserted in the same transaction, so replay after partial success classifies existing refs as `skip_already_imported` and creates only missing descendants. Scanner/Dokploy failures stay retryable unless cancellation interrupted the context; malformed/cross-tenant/stale desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000039-20260518-235141.log:753:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
