ts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000039-20260518-235141.log:830:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000039-20260518-235141.log:831:- Cryptographic-primitive rotation contract pin (BE-0351 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{key_rotation_static_test.go, key_rotation_test.go} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/secrets/AGENTS.md "Key rotation" section; structural template for any future security-verification story over a long-lived cryptographic primitive that ships a rotation surface — JWT signing keys, webhook HMAC secrets, backup-target keys, mTLS issuance keys). Three load-bearing rules: (1) The cryptographic surface is pinned by a static AST analyser, not just by behavioural tests: parse the implementation file, locate the surface methods by name (`NewAESGCM`, `ActiveKeyID`, `KeyIDs`, `Open` for AESGCM), and assert each one's structural shape — `NewAESGCM` must take `[][]byte` (not a single key), `Open` must contain a range loop over the receiver's `keys` slice (not a single-key decrypt), `Open` must return the typed `ErrUnknownKey` identifier (not a string error). Behavioural tests catch the runtime regression but a static guard catches the silent refactor before the runtime test has a chance to fail. The self-check pins both directions of every analyser with synthetic regression sources. (2) The runtime test mirrors the operator runbook line-for-line as a three-phase rotation lifecycle: pre-rotation provider seals under k_old; mid-rotation provider boots with `[k_new, k_old]` and proves (a) old ciphertext still Opens via the retired key id, (b) new Seals reference the new active key id, (c) a fresh Seal under k_new does NOT decrypt under k_old's id (returns ErrInvalidCiphertext); re-seal step (the rotation worker's loop body) Opens under retired key then Seals under active and proves round-trip plaintext intact; post-retirement provider boots with `[k_new]` only and proves a row missed by the re-seal worker surfaces ErrUnknownKey (NOT ErrInvalidCiphertext, NOT a silent plaintext recovery). The runtime test reads as the runbook does so a future operator can compare the runbook against the executable contract. (3) The redacted-projection invariant is captured at runtime with a real `slog.NewJSONHandler` writing into a `bytes.Buffer`: `logger.LogAttrs(..., slog.Any("provider", provider))`, `json.Unmarshal` the result, assert `provider.master_keys == output.Sentinel`. The leak check substring-searches the JSON record for the FULL hex of every probe (k_old, k_new, plaintext, pre/mid ciphertext) — per-byte substring checks false-positive against the SHA-256-prefix key id (the 16-char id will collide with random 2-hex pairs in any 32-byte key at ~p=1/256 per byte). The same probes are checked against `fmt.Sprintf("%s", provider)` to pin the fmt.Stringer channel (panic messages). Both channels MUST also contain at least one accepted key id — the operator's only confirmation that a rotation took effect. (4) The store-layer routing index that the rotation worker depends on is pinned by a SEPARATE sealing trap in the migration package (the `migrations/` directory contains only .sql files — no Go package — so the trap lives in `internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go` and reads each migration via `fs.ReadFile(embeddedMigrations, ...)`). The regex matcher tolerates harmless variations (`IF NOT EXISTS`, optional whitespace, multi-line) but pins the load-bearing parts: index name, table, column order `(secret_provider, secret_key_id)`, and `WHERE is_secret = true` partial-index predicate. A migration that drops the WHERE clause widens the index from "secret rows only" to "every row", which changes the index's purpose and breaks the worker's bounded lookup; the self-check exercises eight known-bad shapes to pin this. The `requiredVariableMigrations` slice is the canonical set of `*_variables` tables — adding a new variable table without adding it here silently reduces coverage.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000039-20260518-235141.log:837:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfigur