./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:656:- Worker Dokploy import jobs (BE-0502 pattern for `import_dokploy_resource`) are organization-scoped only and use a typed payload `(organization_id, yalla_organization_id, dokploy_organization_id[, assignment_dokploy_org_id])`. The worker reloads the Yalla organization from Postgres, rejects stale desired versions before scanning Dokploy, then delegates classification/apply to `migrateimport.Importer` through a store-backed repository adapter. Each imported project/environment/service row and its `dokploy_refs` mapping are inserted in the same transaction, so replay after partial success classifies existing refs as `skip_already_imported` and creates only missing descendants. Scanner/Dokploy failures stay retryable unless cancellation interrupted the context; malformed/cross-tenant/stale desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:716:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:718:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:734:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:741:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:793:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails);