./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:794:- Cryptographic-primitive rotation contract pin (BE-0351 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{key_rotation_static_test.go, key_rotation_test.go} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/secrets/AGENTS.md "Key rotation" section; structural template for any future security-verification story over a long-lived cryptographic primitive that ships a rotation surface — JWT signing keys, webhook HMAC secrets, backup-target keys, mTLS issuance keys). Three load-bearing rules: (1) The cryptographic surface is pinned by a static AST analyser, not just by behavioural tests: parse the implementation file, locate the surface methods by name (`NewAESGCM`, `ActiveKeyID`, `KeyIDs`, `Open` for AESGCM), and assert each one's structural shape — `NewAESGCM` must take `[][]byte` (not a single key), `Open` must contain a range loop over the receiver's `keys` slice (not a single-key decrypt), `Open` must return the typed `ErrUnknownKey` identifier (not a string error). Behavioural tests catch the runtime regression but a static guard catches the silent refactor before the runtime test has a chance to fail. The self-check pins both directions of every analyser with synthetic regression sources. (2) The runtime test mirrors the operator runbook line-for-line as a three-phase rotation lifecycle: pre-rotation provider seals under k_old; mid-rotation provider boots with `[k_new, k_old]` and proves (a) old ciphertext still Opens via the retired key id, (b) new Seals reference the new active key id, (c) a fresh Seal under k_new does NOT decrypt under k_old's id (returns ErrInvalidCiphertext); re-seal step (the rotation worker's loop body) Opens under retired key then Seals under active and proves round-trip plaintext intact; post-retirement provider boots with `[k_new]` only and proves a row missed by the re-seal worker surfaces ErrUnknownKey (NOT ErrInvalidCiphertext, NOT a silent plaintext recovery). The runtime test reads as the runbook does so a future operator can compare the runbook against the executable contract. (3) The redacted-projection invariant is captured at runtime with a real `slog.NewJSONHandler` writing into a `bytes.Buffer`: `logger.LogAttrs(..., slog.Any("provider", provider))`, `json.Unmarshal` the result, assert `provider.master_keys == output.Sentinel`. The leak check substring-searches the JSON record for the FULL hex of every probe (k_old, k_new, plaintext, pre/mid ciphertext) — per-byte substring checks false-positive against the SHA-256-prefix key id (the 16-char id will collide with random 2-hex pairs in any 32-byte key at ~p=1/256 per byte). The same probes are checked against `fmt.Sprintf("%s", provider)` to pin the fmt.Stringer channel (panic messages). Both channels MUST also contain at least one accepted key id — the operator's only confirmation that a rotation took effect. (4) The store-layer routing index that the rotation worker depends on is pinned by a SEPARATE sealing trap in the migration package (the `migrations/` directory contains only .sql files — no Go package — so the trap lives in `internal/controlplane/store/migrate/secret_routing_idx_static_test.go` and reads each migration via `fs.ReadFile(embeddedMigrations, ...)`). The regex matcher tolerates harmless variations (`IF NOT EXISTS`, optional whitespace, multi-line) but pins the load-bearing parts: index name, table, column order `(secret_provider, secret_key_id)`, and `WHERE is_secret = true` partial-index predicate. A migration that drops the WHERE clause widens the index from "secret rows only" to "every row", which changes the index's purpose and breaks the worker's bounded lookup; the self-check exercises eight known-bad shapes to pin this. The `requiredVariableMigrations` slice is the canonical set of `*_variables` tables — adding a new variable table without adding it here silently reduces coverage.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:800:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfigur