./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000133-20260519-130101.log:816:- Cross-cutting policy package = retention-shaped leaf (BE-0038 pattern, internal/controlplane/retention/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, policy.go, policy_test.go}, third instance of the pure-package layout after reconcile and migrateimport): canonical shape for ANY cross-cutting authority over a closed-set domain enum + a validated config + boundary-style predicates — retention windows, rate-limit tiers, idempotency horizons, audit-redaction policies, plan-limit ladders, etc. Layout: doc.go (boundary + the invariants the predicates encode), policy.go (closed-set ResourceKind + a Window struct of just-durations + an immutable Policy value with a private map; DefaultPolicy() returning the production values + NewPolicy(map) that validates every kind is present and every Window passes validate() + copies the input map so caller mutation cannot tamper with a live Policy + Window/Restorable/RestorableAt/RestoreDeadline/RemainingRestore/ReapableAt/ReapDeadline/AllowsSlugReuse predicates over (kind, stampedAt, now)), policy_test.go (every kind × every transition + boundary case + zero-value-Policy safety + NewPolicy validation table + caller-mutation defense + cross-predicate coherence (restorable → held → reapable monotone) + closed-set guard via AllKinds()), AGENTS.md (boundary + invariants). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the boundary comparisons MUST be strict-less-than at the upper bound of RestorableAt and `>=` at the lower bound of ReapableAt so the two predicates are mutually exclusive at the exact instant of transition (a row never reports as both restorable and reapable at the same nanosecond); (2) the package is a LEAF — it imports only std-lib (time, fmt, sort, errors-only-from-tests) and is imported by store/httpapi/worker, so its predicates are cheap to call from anywhere without dragging a database or network dependency along; never let retention import apierr/store/audit; (3) "soft delete where safe" in the B